// 085 — Loop with else clause (Python-style) — DESIGN NOTE, what not to do
//
// Python: `for … else: …` runs the else block only if the loop
// completes without hitting `break`. Novel idea. Also: everyone who
// meets it for the first time reads it as "else when the loop was
// empty" (wrong), or "else as the false branch of the loop's
// condition" (also wrong). The keyword doesn't mean what everyone
// thinks it means, and you have to memorize the rule.
//
// AiLang has no loop-else. Use an explicit sentinel — one boolean,
// named for what it tracks, checked after the loop. The reader knows
// at first glance what "found" means; the reader does not know at
// first glance what `for…else` does.
SubRoutine.Main {
    target = 7
    found = 0
    i = 0
    WhileLoop LessThan(i, 10) {
        IfCondition EqualTo(i, target) ThenBlock: {
            found = 1
            BreakLoop
        }
        i = Add(i, 1)
    }

    IfCondition EqualTo(found, 1) ThenBlock: {
        PrintMessage("found target\n")
    } ElseBlock: {
        PrintMessage("target not found in range\n")
    }
}
RunTask(Main)
